WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.337
Federal compliance is usually
described as a list of obligations.

2
00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:08.703
What an authorizing official needs is
narrower: to know that each control

3
00:00:08.703 --> 00:00:12.638
is working today, and to be able to
show anyone who asks how they know.

4
00:00:12.638 --> 00:00:15.187
That's the difference
between a promise and proof.

5
00:00:15.187 --> 00:00:17.850
A promise is a statement
in the security plan,

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.951
backed by evidence someone gathered
by hand for the last assessment.

7
00:00:21.951 --> 00:00:25.600
It was true the day it was signed,
and it has been aging since.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.219
Proof is a record the
system itself produces,

9
00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:31.900
which another reviewer can check
again and get the same answer.

10
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.350
So start by asking who owes which proof.

11
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.900
The cloud provider proves its
service under FedRAMP's 2026 rules.

12
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.130
The agency proves its own system
and its side of the boundary:

13
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.937
its configuration, identities,
data and integrations.

14
00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:51.053
FedRAMP is plain that each agency
still authorizes its own systems,

15
00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:55.388
and should never issue a standalone
authorization for a cloud service.

16
00:00:55.388 --> 00:01:00.354
OMB's M-24-15 gives a provider's
package a presumption of adequacy,

17
00:01:00.354 --> 00:01:05.413
but the authorizing official must
still issue an authorization.

18
00:01:05.413 --> 00:01:09.580
And only that official decides whether
the remaining risk is acceptable.
